</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>StepFun  Beijing Key Laboratory of Super Intelligent Security of Multi-Modal Information
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Despite advancements in generating visually stunning content, video diffusion models (VDMs) often yield physically inconsistent results due to pixel-only reconstruction.
To address this, we propose MMPhysVideo, the first framework to scale physical plausibility in video generation through joint multimodal modeling. We recast perceptual cues, specifically semantics, geometry, and spatio-temporal trajectory, into a unified pseudo-RGB format, enabling VDMs to directly capture complex physical dynamics.
To mitigate cross-modal interference, we propose a Bidirectionally Controlled Teacher architecture, which utilizes parallel branches to fully decouple RGB and perception processing and adopts two zero-initialized control links to gradually learn pixel-wise consistency. For inference efficiency, the teacher’s physical prior is distilled into a single-stream student model via representation alignment. Furthermore, we present MMPhysPipe, a scalable data curation and annotation pipeline tailored for constructing physics-rich multimodal datasets.
MMPhysPipe employs a vision-language model (VLM) guided by a chain-of-visual-evidence rule to pinpoint physical subjects, enabling expert models to extract multi-granular perceptual information.
Without additional inference costs, MMPhysVideo consistently improves physical plausibility and visual quality over advanced models across various benchmarks and achieves state-of-the-art performance compared to existing methods. <a href="https://shubolin028.github.io/MMPhysVideo-Page" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href" style="--ltx-fg-color:#CC0066;">MMPhysVideo Project Page</a>.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="p1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_top">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:24.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fueled by the scaling of compute and data, video generation models have advanced rapidly <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wan et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peng et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025c</a>)</cite>, enabling a wide range of applications in content creation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and embodied agents <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fu et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">NVIDIA et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Despite remarkable progress in visual aesthetics and fidelity, the lack of physical plausibility in synthesized videos remains a critical bottleneck in the pursuit of real-world simulators.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To align visual outputs with physical principles, current studies focus on the integration of physical priors into text-to-video (T2V) generators. One technical route encodes structured physical priors as prompts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xue et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025c</a>)</cite> or guidance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yuan et al., 2026a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2026</a>)</cite> for T2V generators. However, a significant domain gap exists when mapping textual physical descriptions to pixel-level distribution sampling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025g</a>)</cite>. Another technical route follows a post-tuning paradigm, which implicitly transfers knowledge from foundation models into generators via supervised fine-tuning (SFT) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025g</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025b</a>)</cite> or reinforcement learning (RL) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cai et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. For instance, VideoREPA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025b</a>)</cite> aligns the representations of self-supervised foundation models like DINO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Oquab et al., 2024</a>)</cite> or VideoMAE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tong et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023</a>)</cite> with video diffusion models (VDMs) through distillation-based SFT. While effective, the relation between self-supervised representations and abstract physics principles remains tenuous. Alternatively, PhysHPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025a</a>)</cite> introduces hierarchical preference optimization guided by vision–language models (VLMs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Nonetheless, such RL-based methods rely on complex reward design, which often suffer from reward hacking <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and prohibitive training costs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cai et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. These limitations arise a question: <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Is there a more robust mechanism for integrating physical priors into video generators that ensures explicit correspondence with specific physical content?</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2604.02817v1/overall_workflow_cw.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:537/155;" width="537" height="155" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span><span id="S1.F1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Overall framework of MMPhysVideo.</span> <span id="S1.F1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Left</span>: Our two-stage training framework, which starts with teacher models of parallel branches for joint modeling. Then, we distill a single-stream student model through representation alignment. <span id="S1.F1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Right</span>: Our data engine, MMPhysPipe, for physics data curation and multimodal annotation.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We show the answer is affirmative by recasting perceptual cues, e.g. segmentation, geometry, as pseudo-RGB frames. This strategy provides a data format inherently compatible with the VDMs, empowering them to perform generative world modeling after mastering the perception and understanding of the physical world.
To achieve this goal, we identify and target two challenges:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Designing an effective <span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#789440;">joint modeling architecture</span> to enhance physical consistency in generated videos. While existing diffusion-based perception methods typically employ a single-stream architecture via channel-wise <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2025b</a>)</cite> or spatial-wise <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib82" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025b</a>)</cite> concatenation, directly adopting such designs leads to suboptimal performance on physics-centric benchmarks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib84" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bansal et al., 2024</a>)</cite> as shown in Tab. <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.3 Ablations and Analysis ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ MMPhysVideo: Scaling Physical Plausibility in Video Generation via Joint Multimodal Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Exploiting <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#7E649E;">physics-driven multimodal datasets</span>. Unlike classic post tuning methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cai et al., 2026</a>)</cite> or conditional generation tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Geng et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>, our joint world modeling requires real-world videos with both rich physical phenomena and paired perceptual annotations capturing multi-granular physical dynamics.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To thoroughly tackle these challenges, we propose <span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MMPhysVideo</span>, a framework designed to enhance the physical plausibility of video generation through joint multimodal training. MMPhysVideo starts with a Bidirectionally Controlled Teacher (BCT) model that processes RGB and perceptual modalities in a decoupled manner. To minimize inter-modal interference, we distribute the two modalities into weight-shared parallel branches, ensuring that each modality utilizes an independent computational stream. We then incorporate bidirectional control links with zero-initialization to gradually learn pixel-wise alignment between RGB and perception modalities. To avoid additional computational overhead, the teacher model is distilled into a single-stream student via relation-based representation alignment.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, we propose a physics-centric data curation and multimodal annotation pipeline, <span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MMPhysPipe</span>. Concretely, MMPhysPipe employs a VLM to categorize the physical phenomena in each video and generate the corresponding subject descriptions, which provide a chain-of-visual-evidence reasoning rule for the VLM to assess the physical richness score of each video. Subsequently, MMPhysPipe leverages the grounding capabilities of <span id="S1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">segment anything model</span> (SAM3) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Carion et al., 2026</a>)</cite> to translate these subject descriptions into semantic masks. Finally, to capture spatial geometry and fine-grained dynamics, MMPhysPipe performs pointmap (XYZ) annotation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025b</a>)</cite> and 3D point tracking <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiao et al., 2025</a>)</cite> within the masked regions. This multimodal mix effectively provides the dense perception supervision necessary for a world model to generate consistent physical interactions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, our main contributions are as follows:
<span id="S1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(1)</span> We introduce MMPhysVideo, the first post-tuning framework that enhances the physical plausibility of video synthesis by joint multimodal modeling. MMPhysVideo adopts a Bidirectionally Controlled Teacher (BCT) architecture to preserve video priors while effectively learning physical perception dynamics. To maintain inference efficiency, we distill the teacher’s prior into a single-stream student via relation-based representation alignment.
<span id="S1.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(2)</span> We present MMPhysPipe, a comprehensive data curation and annotation pipeline to construct MMPhysData-36k with physics-rich multimodal datasets.
<span id="S1.p7.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(3)</span> Quantitative and qualitative results show that MMPhysVideo brings remarkable improvements in physical plausibility over various advanced models (i.e., CogVideoX <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025c</a>)</cite>, Wan 2.1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wan et al., 2025</a>)</cite>) and surpasses state-of-the-art physical-target methods on physics-centric benchmarks (i.e., VideoPhy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib84" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bansal et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, PhyGenBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib85" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meng et al., 2024</a>)</cite>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Works</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Physics-aware Video Generation.</span> Benefiting from scaled data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Blattmann et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peng et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and advanced architectures <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ma et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peebles and Xie, 2023</a>)</cite>, video diffusion models (VDMs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qin et al., 2025</a>)</cite> have made significant strides in visual fidelity and sensory realism <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025c</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wan et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ma et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025f</a>)</cite>. However, physical consistency remains a formidable challenge even for state-of-the-art models, as noted by users and benchmarks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib84" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bansal et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib85" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meng et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Motamed et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib83" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>. While existing methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xie et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tan et al., 2024</a>)</cite> leverage physics simulators to generate physics-grounded videos, this technical route is often bottlenecked by the simulator’s narrow scope and the inherent sim-to-real gap. Recent research has shifted toward integrating physical priors within VDMs. One paradigm treats physical knowledge as prompts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xue et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025c</a>)</cite> or guidance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yuan et al., 2026b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yuan et al., 2026a</a>)</cite> to activate the physics potential in VDMs. Another paradigm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cai et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025g</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025b</a>)</cite> utilizes post-tuning to transfer physical insights from foundation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Oquab et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023</a>)</cite> or specialized <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Teed and Deng, 2020</a>)</cite> models to VDMs. This paper, from a distinct perspective, enables generative models to inherently internalize physical dynamics through joint RGB-perception modeling.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Diffusion Models for Perceptual Predictions.</span> Beyond synthesis, another line of works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib80" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ravishankar et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib81" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2025a</a>)</cite> utilize diffusion models for various perception-related tasks. Notably, Marigold <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ke et al., 2024</a>)</cite> pioneered the encoding of depth data as pseudo-RGB images to enable generative perceptual prediction. Following this idea, VDMs have been generalized by subsequent works to a broader range of perceptual modalities, including segmentation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025d</a>; <a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, surface normals <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bin et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, and geometric estimation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib78" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mi et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Recently, several works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chefer et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib77" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xi et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib79" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xi et al., 2025b</a>)</cite> have focused on joint mappings between perceptual conditions and video, achieving multimodal generation. However, simply stacking these modalities often leads to inter-modal interference, which degrades the quality of the generated RGB video. In contrast, this paper explores how joint multimodal modeling can be used to enhance the physical consistency of RGB videos while maintaining visual fidelity.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Method</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Preliminaries: Video Diffusion Models</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Video diffusion models synthesize content through an iterative denoising process <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Esser et al., 2024</a>)</cite> guided by a learned data distribution. Our proposed MMPhysVideo is built upon latent diffusion models (LDMs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rombach et al., 2022</a>)</cite> with Diffusion Transformer (DiT) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peebles and Xie, 2023</a>)</cite>, which provide great model capacity for multimodal modeling. Given an input video <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{rgb}\in\mathbb{R}^{3\times F\times H\times W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{rgb}\in\mathbb{R}^{3\times F\times H\times W}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> denote the number of frames, height, and width, respectively, the 3D Variational Autoencoder (VAE) first projects it into a latent space <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}^{0}_{rgb}=\mathcal{E}(\mathbf{X}_{rgb})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}^{0}_{rgb}=\mathcal{E}(\mathbf{X}_{rgb})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}^{0}_{rgb}\in\mathbb{R}^{c\times f\times h\times w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}^{0}_{rgb}\in\mathbb{R}^{c\times f\times h\times w}</annotation></semantics></math>. Here, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> represents the embedding dimensions of 3D VAE, while <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> denote the compressed temporal duration, height, and width. During training, a timestep <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\sim\mathcal{U}(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\sim\mathcal{U}(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> and Gaussian noise <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{rgb}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐈</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{rgb}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I})</annotation></semantics></math> are sampled to produce a noisy latent <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}^{t}_{rgb}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}^{t}_{rgb}</annotation></semantics></math> via <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}^{t}_{rgb}=\mathbf{z}^{0}_{rgb}+\sigma_{t}^{2}\epsilon_{rgb}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}^{t}_{rgb}=\mathbf{z}^{0}_{rgb}+\sigma_{t}^{2}\epsilon_{rgb}</annotation></semantics></math> with variance <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The denoiser <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u(\cdot;\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u(\cdot;\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> is optimized to minimize the noise estimation loss:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}=\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{z}^{0}_{rgb},\epsilon\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I}),t\sim\mathcal{U}(0,1)}\left[\|u(\mathbf{z}^{t}_{rgb},y,t;\theta)-\epsilon_{rgb}\|^{2}\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐈</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}=\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{z}^{0}_{rgb},\epsilon\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I}),t\sim\mathcal{U}(0,1)}\left[\|u(\mathbf{z}^{t}_{rgb},y,t;\theta)-\epsilon_{rgb}\|^{2}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> is the conditioning signal (e.g., text prompt), <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the weights, and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> is the denoiser’s prediction. During inference, the denoised latent <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{z}}^{0}_{rgb}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{z}}^{0}_{rgb}</annotation></semantics></math> is mapped back to pixel space via the VAE decoder <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> to yield the final video <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{X}}_{rgb}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{X}}_{rgb}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>MMPhysVideo: From Perception to Generation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">MMPhysVideo extends video diffusion models (VDMs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qin et al., 2025</a>)</cite> toward joint RGB and perception modeling to better capture the physical dynamics of the real world. Existing VDMs often suffer from artifacts such as semantic ambiguities, flickering scene structures, and inconsistent temporal deformations, all of which compromise the physical realism of generated videos. To address these issues, we incorporate three critical perceptual cues: semantics, geometry, and spatio-temporal trajectory.
To improve training efficiency, we consolidate these multimodal signals into a unified pseudo-RGB video representation, <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{percep}\in\mathbb{R}^{3\times F\times H\times W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{percep}\in\mathbb{R}^{3\times F\times H\times W}</annotation></semantics></math>, as detailed in Sec. <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 MMPhysPipe ‣ 3 Method ‣ MMPhysVideo: Scaling Physical Plausibility in Video Generation via Joint Multimodal Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2604.02817v1/arch_comparison_cw.svg" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:527/193;" width="527" height="193"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span><span id="S3.F2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Architecture comparison.</span> <span id="S3.F2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Left</span>: Channel-wise fusion used in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xi et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chefer et al., 2025</a>)</cite> <span id="S3.F2.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Middle</span> Spatial-wise fusion used in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib82" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025b</a>)</cite> <span id="S3.F2.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Right</span>: Our decoupled design with pixel-wise fusion.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.1 </span>Joint Multimodal Modeling</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a video sequence and its paired perceptual video <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{percep}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{percep}</annotation></semantics></math>, we follow the LDMs paradigm to unify multimodal tasks:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\epsilon}_{rgb},\hat{\epsilon}_{percep}=u(\mathbf{z}^{t}_{rgb},\mathbf{z}^{t}_{percep},y,t;\theta)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\epsilon}_{rgb},\hat{\epsilon}_{percep}=u(\mathbf{z}^{t}_{rgb},\mathbf{z}^{t}_{percep},y,t;\theta)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}^{t}_{percep}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}^{t}_{percep}</annotation></semantics></math> is the perceptual latent encoded by 3D VAE, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\epsilon}_{rgb}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\epsilon}_{rgb}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\epsilon}_{percep}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\epsilon}_{percep}</annotation></semantics></math> represent the predicted noise for RGB and perceptual modalities, respectively. Unlike methods using perceptual cues as conditional inputs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Geng et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025b</a>)</cite> or post-hoc estimates <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, our joint modeling paradigm generates both from noise to foster inherent perceptual understanding.
Our experiments in Sec. <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 Ablations and Analysis ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ MMPhysVideo: Scaling Physical Plausibility in Video Generation via Joint Multimodal Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> reveal that the model architecture for joint generation is crucial for overall performance. Consequently, we analyze several architecture design from the fields of perception and controllable generation, alongside our proposed decoupled architecture.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Channel-wise Concatenation.</span> Most attempts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chefer et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib77" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025a</a>)</cite> concatenate RGB and perceptual latents along the channel dimension, expanding the channels of input layer via zero-initialization to avoid training collapse. While this maintains a constant sequence length, premature fusion and zero-initialized weights often degrade perceptual representation, yielding blurred results as shown in Fig. <a href="#S4.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ Table 2 ‣ 4.3 Ablations and Analysis ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ MMPhysVideo: Scaling Physical Plausibility in Video Generation via Joint Multimodal Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Spatial-wise Concatenation.</span> Recent works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ju et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tan et al., 2025</a>)</cite> concatenate latents along spatial dimensions (i.e., height or width), patchifying modalities independently and using 3D RoPE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">Su et al., 2024</a>)</cite> for alignment. However, non-pixel-aligned interactions within the self-attention mechanism of DiT blocks struggle with high fidelity and the extended sequence length increases computational overhead.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">These <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">single-stream architectures</span>, which rely on concatenation-based fusion, fail to decouple the distinct features of each modality, leading to inter-modal interference with suboptimal performance.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2604.02817v1/workflow_MMPhysVideo_cw.png" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:537/398;" width="537" height="398" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span><span id="S3.F3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Overview of MMPhysVideo.</span> <span id="S3.F3.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Stage <math id="S3.F3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math></span>: A dual-stream teacher model with parallel branches is first trained to handle RGB and perception modalities concurrently. Then, we use bidirectional control links to enable pixel-wise alignment. <span id="S3.F3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Stage <math id="S3.F3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="II" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">II</annotation></semantics></math></span>: For inference efficiency, we distill a single-stream student model through relation alginment.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.2 </span>Bidirectionally Controlled Teacher Model</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We address these issues in a divide-and-conquer manner. We first fully decouple the computation of RGB and perceptual modalities through parallel branches to prevent modal confusion. Then, we introduce bidirectional control links to establish pixel-wise correspondences between the modalities.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Parallel Branches.</span> Building upon standard RGB processing, we construct a parallel computational branch for the perceptual modality, forming a <span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">dual-stream architecture</span>. Instead of duplicating all DiT parameters, two branches share model weights while keeping the forward computation independent. This design preserves the pre-trained RGB video priors while seamlessly extending VDMs to perceptual video generation. To further enforce modal discriminability, we introduce learnable task embeddings, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{rgb}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{rgb}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{percep}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{percep}</annotation></semantics></math>, which are integrated into the diffusion timestep embedding <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e(t;\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e(t;\theta)</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{rgb}=e_{rgb}+e(t;\theta),\quad t_{percep}=e_{percep}+e(t;\theta)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{rgb}=e_{rgb}+e(t;\theta),\quad t_{percep}=e_{percep}+e(t;\theta)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Similarly, we also apply a distinct set of learnable task embeddings to the input latents. Then, the parallel computation branches can be formulated as:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{z}}_{rgb}={\rm DiTBlocks}(\mathbf{z}_{rgb},t_{rgb}),\quad\hat{\mathbf{z}}_{percep}={\rm DiTBlocks}(\mathbf{z}_{percep},t_{percep})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>DiTBlocks</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>DiTBlocks</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{z}}_{rgb}={\rm DiTBlocks}(\mathbf{z}_{rgb},t_{rgb}),\quad\hat{\mathbf{z}}_{percep}={\rm DiTBlocks}(\mathbf{z}_{percep},t_{percep})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">By isolating the computation, the DiT model can independently master the generation of the newly introduced perceptual modality, thereby reducing the overall optimization difficulty.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bidirectional Control Links.</span> To capture fine-grained physical dynamics, perceptual modalities such as 3D point tracking should maintain pixel-wise consistency with the RGB stream. Beyond simply training on paired data, we explicitly establish cross-modal alignment by introducing bidirectional control links within corresponding DiT blocks, which facilitates direct pixel-wise interaction between the RGB and perceptual representations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For a DiT model consisting of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> blocks, we define a subset of indices <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}\subseteq\{1,\dots,K\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}\subseteq\{1,\dots,K\}</annotation></semantics></math> for link insertion. For <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>-th selected block (<math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\in\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\in\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>), we inject the intermediate representations from one branch into the other to update the hidden states, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbf{z}}^{[l]}_{rgb}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbf{z}}^{[l]}_{rgb}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbf{z}}^{[l]}_{percep}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbf{z}}^{[l]}_{percep}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is formulated as:</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbf{z}}^{[l]}_{rgb}=\mathbf{z}^{[l]}_{rgb}+{\rm CtrlLink}_{percep\rightarrow rgb}(\mathbf{z}^{[l]}_{percep}),~\tilde{\mathbf{z}}^{[l]}_{percep}=\mathbf{z}^{[l]}_{percep}+{\rm CtrlLink}_{rgb\rightarrow percep}(\mathbf{z}^{[l]}_{rgb})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>CtrlLink</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.497em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>CtrlLink</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbf{z}}^{[l]}_{rgb}=\mathbf{z}^{[l]}_{rgb}+{\rm CtrlLink}_{percep\rightarrow rgb}(\mathbf{z}^{[l]}_{percep}),~\tilde{\mathbf{z}}^{[l]}_{percep}=\mathbf{z}^{[l]}_{percep}+{\rm CtrlLink}_{rgb\rightarrow percep}(\mathbf{z}^{[l]}_{rgb})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where each control link is implemented as a linear layer by default. Following the convention of controllable generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, we employ zero initialization for all control links. This strategy minimizes inter-modal interference during the early training phase and enables the model to progressively learn complex, pixel-level correspondences between RGB and perceptual modalities.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.3 </span>Representation alignment</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For inference efficiency, we distill the dual-stream teacher model back into a single-stream student model that processes only the RGB modality. However, directly performing MSE regression on raw features can be restrictive and degrade generation quality <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">Feng et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Inspired by VideoREPA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>, we align the pairwise token similarities between the teacher’s and student’s representations, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbf{z}}^{t}_{rgb}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbf{z}}^{t}_{rgb}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}^{s}_{rgb}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mi>s</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}^{s}_{rgb}</annotation></semantics></math>, across both spatial and temporal dimensions:</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{distill}=|\mathcal{R}_{spa}(\tilde{\mathbf{z}}^{t}_{rgb})-\mathcal{R}_{spa}(h_{\varphi}(\mathbf{z}^{s}_{rgb}))|+|\mathcal{R}_{temp}(\tilde{\mathbf{z}}^{t}_{rgb})-\mathcal{R}_{temp}(h_{\varphi}(\mathbf{z}^{s}_{rgb}))|" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>φ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>φ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{distill}=|\mathcal{R}_{spa}(\tilde{\mathbf{z}}^{t}_{rgb})-\mathcal{R}_{spa}(h_{\varphi}(\mathbf{z}^{s}_{rgb}))|+|\mathcal{R}_{temp}(\tilde{\mathbf{z}}^{t}_{rgb})-\mathcal{R}_{temp}(h_{\varphi}(\mathbf{z}^{s}_{rgb}))|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{spa}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{spa}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{temp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{temp}</annotation></semantics></math> denote functions that compute token relations in the spatial and temporal dimensions, respectively, and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{\varphi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>φ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{\varphi}</annotation></semantics></math> is a trainable MLP projector. Through this objective, the student model learns the spatio-temporal structural dependencies inherent in the teacher’s fused RGB-perceptual representations. Initialized with teacher weights, the student model integrate dual-stream priors of perception and RGB into a single DiT. This allows the single-stream model to internalize physical priors and generate consistent videos without the auxiliary perceptual branch.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2604.02817v1/workflow_MMPhysPipe_cw.png" id="S3.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:537/212;" width="537" height="212" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span><span id="S3.F4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Overview of MMPhysPipe.</span> We employ a VLM, Qwen3-VL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>, to curate videos with rich physical interactions and generate physical subject descriptions following our chain-of-visual-evidence (CoVE) rule. Subsequently, expert perception models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Carion et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiao et al., 2025</a>)</cite> are leveraged to produce multi-granular annotations.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>MMPhysPipe</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To bridge the gap in physics-aware generation via joint multimodal modeling, we introduce a scalable dataset pipeline, MMPhysPipe. MMPhysPipe is designed to overcome two primary bottlenecks: the curation of video samples exhibiting rich physical phenomena from large text-video datasets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nan et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025e</a>; <a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024b</a>)</cite> and the production of high-fidelity, multi-granular perceptual annotations.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.3.1 </span>Data Curation</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a large-scale text-video dataset (we adopt OpenVidHD-0.4M <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nan et al., 2024</a>)</cite> in this work, which is widely utilized for post-tuning), MMPhysPipe first employs a lightweight Video Quality Assessment (VQA) model from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025e</a>)</cite> to efficiently filter out low-quality samples, such as freeze-frame or overexposed videos. The VQA model provides an initial holistic score for each video by integrating multiple dimensions, including dynamics, aesthetics, and clarity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reality.</span> Subsequently, we select raw videos captured by physical cameras in real-world environments using a Vision-Language Model (VLM), i.e., Qwen3-VL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>. We formalize the concept of ”real-world raw videos” through a rigorous set of visual constraints spanning four key dimensions: Style, Context, Graphics, and Temporal Continuity, as shown in Fig. <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3.2.3 Representation alignment ‣ 3.2 MMPhysVideo: From Perception to Generation ‣ 3 Method ‣ MMPhysVideo: Scaling Physical Plausibility in Video Generation via Joint Multimodal Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, which provide explicit evaluation protocol for Chain-of-Thought (CoT) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025a</a>)</cite> reasoning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Physical Richness.</span> Following prior works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025c</a>)</cite>, we categorize physical phenomena into three primary domains, each subdivided into fine-grained primitives, as shown in Fig. <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3.2.3 Representation alignment ‣ 3.2 MMPhysVideo: From Perception to Generation ‣ 3 Method ‣ MMPhysVideo: Scaling Physical Plausibility in Video Generation via Joint Multimodal Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. MMPhysPipe leverages Qwen3-VL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2025a</a>)</cite> as a physical evaluator to assign an intensity score ranging from <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> for each primitive, where <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> denotes a negligible occurrence and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> represents high-impact manifestation. To mitigate hallucinations outputs, we implement a Chain-of-Visual-Evidence (CoVE) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2025b</a>)</cite> reasoning rule: the VLM should articulate specific visual cues, such as momentum exchange in a collision, before quantifying the intensity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Crucially, for primitives achieving the highest intensity scores, the VLM performs <span id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Physical Subject Extraction</span>, generating concise descriptive phrases (e.g., ”steam rising from pan”). These phrases act as semantic anchors, bridging the gap between high-level physical principles and the subsequent low-level perceptual grounding annotation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Resampling.</span>
We introduce a multi-label balanced resampling strategy to ensure dataset diversity.
Given a data pool <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}=\{(v_{i},\mathbf{s}_{i})\}_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}=\{(v_{i},\mathbf{s}_{i})\}_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th video and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}_{i}\in[1,5]^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>M</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}_{i}\in[1,5]^{M}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes an <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional physical richness score vector, we define a threshold <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> to determine the presence of the <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th physical primitive. For samples where no primitive score exceeds <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, we assign the label corresponding to the maximum score, which can be formulated as:</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{cases}\displaystyle y_{ij}=\mathbb{I}(s_{ij}\geq\tau),&amp;\sum_{j=1}^{M}y_{ij}&gt;0\\[15.0pt]
\displaystyle y_{ij^{*}}=1,j^{*}=\arg\max_{j}s_{ij},&amp;\sum_{j=1}^{M}y_{ij}=0\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝕀</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≥</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup></mstyle><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>arg</mi><mi>max</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>s</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup></mstyle><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{cases}\displaystyle y_{ij}=\mathbb{I}(s_{ij}\geq\tau),&amp;\sum_{j=1}^{M}y_{ij}&gt;0\\[15.0pt]
\displaystyle y_{ij^{*}}=1,j^{*}=\arg\max_{j}s_{ij},&amp;\sum_{j=1}^{M}y_{ij}=0\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{I}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕀</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{I}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is the indicator function. Then, we calculate the frequency <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and the Imbalance Ratio per Binary Label <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Thölke et al., 2023</a>)</cite> for each primitive <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> and assign a normalized sampling probability <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(v_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(v_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> to each video:</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm IRBL}_{j}=\frac{\max_{k\in\{1,\dots,M\}}(C_{k})}{C_{j}},\quad P(v_{i})=\frac{\sum_{j=1}^{M}y_{ij}{\rm IRBL}_{j}}{\sum_{j=1}^{M}{\rm IRBL}_{j}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>IRBL</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>IRBL</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>IRBL</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm IRBL}_{j}=\frac{\max_{k\in\{1,\dots,M\}}(C_{k})}{C_{j}},\quad P(v_{i})=\frac{\sum_{j=1}^{M}y_{ij}{\rm IRBL}_{j}}{\sum_{j=1}^{M}{\rm IRBL}_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p5.3" class="ltx_p">Finally, we draw <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> samples from the data pool according to the probability distribution <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> to construct our balanced dataset, MMPhysData-36k. More statistical analysis of the physical primitive distribution can be found in <span id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p5.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;">Appendix</span>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.3.2 </span>Multimodal Annotation</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To provide a comprehensive perception of physical content, we perform multi-granular annotation in a top-down manner, spanning semantic, geometry, and spatiotemporal trajectory.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Instance Segmentation.</span> Based on the physical subject descriptions generated by the VLM, we leverage SAM3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Carion et al., 2026</a>)</cite> with promptable concept segmentation to transfer linguistic noun phrases to semantic masks. By utilizing SAM3’s Presence Head, MMPhysPipe effectively eliminates hallucinated subjects, retaining only masks for physically active subjects.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Geometry and Point Tracking.</span> Within the masked regions, we employ a unified, feed-forward 3D tracker <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiao et al., 2025</a>)</cite> to simultaneously estimate scene geometry and 3D point trajectories <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{\mathbf{p}_{i}(s)\in\mathbb{R}^{3}\}_{s=1}^{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>F</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{p}_{i}(s)\in\mathbb{R}^{3}\}_{s=1}^{F}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> is the frame index. The color <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐫</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of each point is assigned based on its normalized coordinates in the initial frame <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s=1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s=1)</annotation></semantics></math>, where we map the <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y,1/z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y,1/z)</annotation></semantics></math> coordinates into the <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> RGB space. These colors remain constant across all frames to maintain temporal identity. For each frame <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>, the 3D points are projected onto the <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>-th camera plane to encode the final perceptual RGB frame.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Compared with any single perceptual modality, our unified videos with multiple perceptual modalities provides a more holistic scaffold and a continuous information flow from global semantic understanding to local geometric details.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baselines.</span> To assess the generality of our joint multimodal modeling framework, we integrate MMPhysVideo within advanced T2V video diffusion models, Wan2.1-1.3B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wan et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, CogvideoX-5B and 2B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025c</a>)</cite>. For a comprehensive comparison, we adopt several competitive baselines with open-source code: 1) PhyT2V <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xue et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and VideoREPA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>, which specialize in enhancing physical plausibility 2) OmniVDiff <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xi et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>, a single-stream multimodal generation method. Comparison results with other RL-based and guidance-based methods are shown in <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;">Appendix</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Implementation Details.</span> <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Stage <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math></span>. We first train the teacher models on our curated MMPhysData-36k dataset with a global batch size of 16 for 5K steps. We employ the AdamW <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Loshchilov and Hutter, 2017</a>)</cite> optimizer with a constant learning rate of <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times 10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times 10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math>. <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Stage <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="II" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">II</annotation></semantics></math></span>. For the distillation phase, the single-stream student model is initialized with the weights of the pre-trained teacher. We perform representation alignment on the DiT blocks where control links are inserted. The student model is finetuned for 1k steps with a global batch size of 32 and a learning rate of <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times 10^{-6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times 10^{-6}</annotation></semantics></math>, following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>. For the ablation studies, we utilize the CogVideoX-2B backbone under the <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Stage I</span> configuration as the default setting.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evaluation.</span> We evaluate MMPhysVideo on two physics-centric benchmarks: (1) VideoPhy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib84" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bansal et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, which focuses on interactions across solid-solid (SS), solid-fluid (SF), and fluid-fluid (FF) phases. (2) PhyGenBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib85" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meng et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, which covers physical domains including mechanics (Mec), optics (Opt), thermodynamics (Tdm), and material properties (Mat). For quantitative assessment, we employ VideoScore2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2025</a>)</cite> as our primary automated evaluator with metric across <span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Physical Consistency</span> (PC) and <span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Semantic Alignment</span> (SA).
Compared to VideoCon-Physics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib84" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bansal et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, VideoScore2 leverages CoT rule to generate reasons for its scores, which ensures a more robust, human-aligned evaluations. We also provide results using the VideoCon-Physics evaluator, alongside VBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2024</a>)</cite> in <span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;">Appendix</span>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span><span id="S4.T1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results on Videophy and PhyGenBench.</span> We divide the experiment into four groups based on the <span id="S4.T1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#EBEBEB;">backbone</span>. <math id="S4.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dagger" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>†</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dagger</annotation></semantics></math> denotes models with our distillation strategy.</figcaption>
