</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Main results</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Quantitative Comparisons.</span> As shown in Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.1 Experimental Setup ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ MMPhysVideo: Scaling Physical Plausibility in Video Generation via Joint Multimodal Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we conduct a comprehensive quantitative evaluation across multiple base models and physics-centric benchmarks. The experimental results lead to the following key observations: <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1</span>) MMPhysVideo demonstrates remarkable versatility, consistently enhancing physical plausibility across various T2V backbones with different model scales. Specifically, on the VideoPhy and PhyGenBench suites, MMPhysVideo improves the Average PC scores of CogVideoX-5B by <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5.0\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5.0</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5.0\%</annotation></semantics></math>/<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6.2\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>6.2</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6.2\%</annotation></semantics></math>, and Wan2.1-1.3B by <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.2\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.2</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.2\%</annotation></semantics></math>/<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.2\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.2</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.2\%</annotation></semantics></math> respectively. <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2</span>) MMPhysVideo sets a new state-of-the-art for physically consistent video generation, outperforming specialized methods across both benchmarks. For instance, under CogVideoX-5B backbone configuration, MMPhysVideo surpasses PhyT2V and VideoREPA by <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.6\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.6</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.6\%</annotation></semantics></math>/<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5.0\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5.0</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5.0\%</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.2\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.2</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.2\%</annotation></semantics></math>/<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.5\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.5</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.5\%</annotation></semantics></math> in Average PC scores on VideoPhy and PhyGenBench, respectively. <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3</span>) Even when configured with the lighter CogVideoX-2B backbone, MMPhysVideo significantly outperforms the multimodal generation baseline, OmniVDiff. Notably, OmniVDiff utilizes a more powerful backbone (CogVideoX1.5-5B) and a training dataset over <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="10\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10\times</annotation></semantics></math> larger than our MMPhysData-36k.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2604.02817v1/percep_vis_case.png" id="S4.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:537/181;" width="537" height="181" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span><span id="S4.F6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Visualization of joint RGB-perception generation.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Qualitative Comparison.</span> As shown in Fig. <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4.1 Experimental Setup ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ MMPhysVideo: Scaling Physical Plausibility in Video Generation via Joint Multimodal Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, we present qualitative comparisons between MMPhysVideo, baseline T2V generators, and the physics method, VideoREPA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>. Our evaluation spans diverse scenarios involving various physical states and laws. Our MMPhysVideo demonstrates superior physical plausibility across diverse scenarios involving various physical states and laws. Specifically, in the “hand-phone” scenario, MMPhysVideo generates the correct number of fingers and joints while precisely capturing the fine-grained dynamics of screen-touch interactions. In the ”wine-glass” scenario, the baseline generator fails to comprehend the spatial relationship between the bottle and the glass, as well as the causal logic of fluid motion, where the bottle intersects the glass and the liquid flows erratically from the glass onto the table. Conversely, MMPhysVideo accurately captures the fluid trajectory and maintains the structural integrity of the interacting objects. More visual results can be found in <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;">Appendix</span>.</p>
</div>
</section>
