<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7: </span><span id="S4.F7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Qualitative comparison.</span> We select pointmap for better visualization.</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Variants of Architectures for Joint Modeling.</span> In Sec. <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 MMPhysVideo: From Perception to Generation ‣ 3 Method ‣ MMPhysVideo: Scaling Physical Plausibility in Video Generation via Joint Multimodal Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, we introduce a novel dual-stream architecture that leverages bidirectional control links within parallel RGB-perceptual branches to achieve ”pixel-wise” cross-modal interaction. To verify its effectiveness, we conduct a comparative study against alternative fusion strategies, including the ”channel-wise” fusion used in baseline method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xi et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chefer et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and the ”spatial-wise” fusion in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib82" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>. For a fair comparison, we only modify the joint modeling architecture while keeping the training data, and training steps identical. As shown by the qualitative and quantitative results in Fig. <a href="#S4.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ Table 2 ‣ 4.3 Ablations and Analysis ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ MMPhysVideo: Scaling Physical Plausibility in Video Generation via Joint Multimodal Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> and Tab. <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.3 Ablations and Analysis ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ MMPhysVideo: Scaling Physical Plausibility in Video Generation via Joint Multimodal Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, our method yields fine-grained perceptual predictions and significantly enhances the average PC score. Furthermore, we observe that premature modality fusion (e.g., ”channel-wise” fusion) tends to exacerbate inter-modal interference, degrading the quality of both outputs.</p>
</div>
