</section>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>More Details of MMPhysVideo</h2>

<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Training Details</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All experiments are conducted on a single machine equipped with eight NVIDIA L40S GPUs (48GB VRAM each). We perform full-parameter fine-tuning for all models, utilizing the DeepSpeed ZeRO-2 optimization strategy to enhance training efficiency. To incorporate the proposed Bidirectionally Controlled Teacher (BCT), we insert control links within the dual-stream Diffusion Transformer (DiT). Specifically, for the 42-layer Transformer of CogVideoX-5B, the links are placed at blocks <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[3,9,15,21,27,33,39]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>15</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>21</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>27</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>33</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>39</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[3,9,15,21,27,33,39]</annotation></semantics></math>. For the 30-layer Transformers of Wan2.1-1.3B and CogVideoX-2B, we select blocks <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[3,9,15,21,27]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>15</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>21</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>27</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[3,9,15,21,27]</annotation></semantics></math> as insertion points. An additional set of bidirectional control links is implemented prior to the Transformer across all models.
Regarding the data configuration, Wan2.1-1.3B is trained with a resolution of <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="480\times 832" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>480</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>832</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">480\times 832</annotation></semantics></math> and a sequence length of 81 frames at 16 fps. For the CogVideoX series, we adopt a resolution of <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="480\times 720" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>480</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>720</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">480\times 720</annotation></semantics></math> with 49 frames at 8 fps.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Inference Details</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For inference, we employ the DDIM sampler with 50 steps. Benefiting from the proposed relation-based representation distillation, our student model avoid the need for a dual-stream architecture to simultaneously generate RGB and perceptual modalities. As the student model contains no control links, it shares the exact same inference procedure and computational cost as the base model.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>More Details of MMPhysPipe</h2>

<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>Video Quality Assessment</h3>

<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We employ a lightweight Video Quality Assessment (VQA) model from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025e</a>)</cite> to assign an initial holistic score to each video. As shown in Fig. <a href="#A2.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ B.1 Video Quality Assessment ‣ Appendix B More Details of MMPhysPipe ‣ MMPhysVideo: Scaling Physical Plausibility in Video Generation via Joint Multimodal Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, the VQA scores for the OpenVidHD-0.4M dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nan et al., 2024</a>)</cite> are primarily concentrated between <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3.0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4.0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4.0</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, the median score of VQA is <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.62" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3.62</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.62</annotation></semantics></math>, which is significantly higher than the official reference value of 2.5. Consequently, we implement a stringent threshold of <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3.0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.0</annotation></semantics></math> to maintain a high standard for data selection. In Fig. <a href="#A2.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ B.1 Video Quality Assessment ‣ Appendix B More Details of MMPhysPipe ‣ MMPhysVideo: Scaling Physical Plausibility in Video Generation via Joint Multimodal Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>, we also present examples of the VQA scoring process. It is evident that the VQA model effectively assigns lower scores to freeze-frame videos, facilitating their removal.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.F9" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2604.02817v1/vtss_433k_analysis.png" id="A2.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:537/202;" width="537" height="202" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 9: </span><span id="A2.F9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Analysis of VQA Score Distribution</span> with frequency distribution (left) and the cumulative proportion (right), respectively. </figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A2.F10" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2604.02817v1/vqa_examples.png" id="A2.F10.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:537/123;" width="537" height="123" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 10: </span><span id="A2.F10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Visualization results</span> of VQA scoring.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>Reality</h3>

<div id="A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To exclude synthetic content such as animations and CGI, we establish a rigorous definition of ”real-world raw videos” across four distinct dimensions. Our meticulously crafted prompt for assessing reality is illustrated in Fig. <a href="#A2.F11" title="Figure 11 ‣ B.3 Physical Richness ‣ Appendix B More Details of MMPhysPipe ‣ MMPhysVideo: Scaling Physical Plausibility in Video Generation via Joint Multimodal Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>. Leveraging Qwen-VL-30B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>, we implement a Chain-of-Thought (CoT) reasoning method to assign scores ranging from 1 to 5. Subsequently, only videos with a reality score of <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> or higher are retained to ensure high reality fidelity. The CoT reasoning provides the model with more explicit evaluation criteria, offering superior interpretability compared to direct scoring. As shown in Fig. <a href="#A2.F12" title="Figure 12 ‣ B.3 Physical Richness ‣ Appendix B More Details of MMPhysPipe ‣ MMPhysVideo: Scaling Physical Plausibility in Video Generation via Joint Multimodal Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>, our CoT-driven scoring precisely identifies digital graphic and post-produced segments that are typically overlooked by direct scoring.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.3 </span>Physical Richness</h3>

<div id="A2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following the taxonomy established in WISA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025c</a>)</cite>, we categorize physical phenomena into three primary domains: Dynamics, Thermodynamics, and Optics. Each domain is further subdivided into a set of fine-grained physical primitives, as detailed below: (1) <span id="A2.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dynamic</span>: <span id="A2.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">rigid body motion, collision, liquid motion, gas motion, elastic motion, deformation.</span> (2) <span id="A2.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Thermodynamic</span>: <span id="A2.SS3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">melting, solidification, vaporization, liquefaction, combustion, explosion.</span> (3) <span id="A2.SS3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Optic</span>: <span id="A2.SS3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">reflection, refraction, scattering, interference and diffraction, unnatural light source</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The final Physical Richness score is derived by aggregating the scores across these three domains. Taking the Dynamic domain as an illustration: for a given video, we first compute the mean intensity score across all Dynamic primitives to obtain the domain-specific score, denoted as <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{d}\in[1,5]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{d}\in[1,5]</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly, the Thermodynamics score <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and Optics score <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{o}</annotation></semantics></math> are obtained through the same procedure. Finally, the total Physical Richness score is calculated as the average of the scores from all three domains.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.F11" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2604.02817v1/reality_prompt.png" id="A2.F11.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:521/277;" width="521" height="277" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 11: </span><span id="A2.F11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Demonstration of the crafted prompt</span> for reality score.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A2.F12" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2604.02817v1/reality_examples.png" id="A2.F12.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:521/223;" width="521" height="223" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 12: </span><span id="A2.F12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Visualization results</span> of CoT reality scoring.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.4 </span>Resampling</h3>

<div id="A2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following the label assignment strategy defined in Eq. 9, we conduct a statistical analysis of the physical primitive distribution across three distinct domains. As shown in the top panel of Fig. <a href="#A2.F13" title="Figure 13 ‣ B.4 Resampling ‣ Appendix B More Details of MMPhysPipe ‣ MMPhysVideo: Scaling Physical Plausibility in Video Generation via Joint Multimodal Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>, the initial distribution reveals a significant long-tail effect: videos are heavily clustered in common primitives such as <span id="A2.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">rigid body motion</span> and <span id="A2.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">reflection</span>, leading to substantial content redundancy. Conversely, more complex physical phenomena, including liquid motion and solidification, remain underrepresented. To construct a more balanced physical dataset, we introduce a multi-label resampling strategy. The resulting distribution after resampling is shown in the bottom panel of Fig. <a href="#A2.F13" title="Figure 13 ‣ B.4 Resampling ‣ Appendix B More Details of MMPhysPipe ‣ MMPhysVideo: Scaling Physical Plausibility in Video Generation via Joint Multimodal Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>, where the proportion of rare physical primitive labels has been significantly augmented, ensuring a more diverse representation of physical dynamics.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.F13" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2604.02817v1/ditribution_resample.png" id="A2.F13.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:537/211;" width="537" height="211" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 13: </span><span id="A2.F13.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Analysis of physical phenomenon distribution</span> before (<span id="A2.F13.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">top</span>) and after (<span id="A2.F13.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">bottom</span>) applying resampling strategy.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.5 </span>Multimodal Annotation</h3>

<div id="A2.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a video, the physical subjects descriptions from the VLM are processed by SAM3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Carion et al., 2026</a>)</cite> for open-vocabulary segmentation. Subsequently, 2,500 points are uniformly sampled from the union of all masked regions and projected into 3D space using the camera intrinsic and extrinsic parameters estimated by VGGT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>. Finally, we employ SpatialTrackerv2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiao et al., 2025</a>)</cite> to perform point tracking on these uniformly distributed 3D points, which are then re-projected onto the 2D plane as video sequences. In these sequences, the point size is dynamically adjusted to accommodate varying levels of granularity.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.T5" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center ltx_align_floatright" style="width:44%;">
<br class="ltx_break ltx_break">
<table id="A2.T5.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="A2.T5.1.1" class="ltx_tr" style="--ltx-bg-color:#CCD1D1;">
<th id="A2.T5.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:5.0pt;padding-right:5.0pt;"><span id="A2.T5.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-bg-color:#CCD1D1;">Component</span></th>
